---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro'
import { failedPatterns, totalPatterns } from '../lib/data.ts'
import { int } from '../lib/html.ts'

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'at-build-time', text: 'At build time' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'at-run-time', text: 'At run time' },
]
---

<Doc headings={headings}>
  <p>The heavy work happens when the package is built. The browser gets one compact file per language and a small scanner.</p>

  <h2 id="at-build-time">At build time</h2>
  <ol class="pipeline">
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Resolve</strong>
        <p>References between grammar rules become rule numbers.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Convert</strong>
        <p>
          Each regex is rewritten from Oniguruma to JavaScript.{' '}
          {
            failedPatterns
              ? `${int(totalPatterns - failedPatterns)} of ${int(totalPatterns)} convert.`
              : `All ${int(totalPatterns)} convert.`
          }
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Intern</strong>
        <p>Repeated scope names and regexes are stored once.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Encode</strong>
        <p>The rules are packed into numbers, one ES module per language.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="at-run-time">At run time</h2>
  <ol class="pipeline" style="counter-reset: step 4">
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Scan</strong>
        <p>
          A rule's patterns are joined into one regex, so a single search finds the next match. Patterns that need the
          <code>v</code> or <code>i</code> flag run on their own and tie with it by rule order.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="pipeline-text">
        <strong>Walk</strong>
        <p>The TextMate loop turns those matches into tokens, line by line.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</Doc>
